"""Most of the table's codes for these instances are not at a local optimum of the largest cosine. Local search from
the published coordinates lowered the cosine of 40 of the 49 by more than 1e-12 and of 28 by more than 1e-6, by up
to 2.6e-04 (9_223); 40 of them beat the table's displayed (rounded-up) cosine by at least 1e-12. The search per
instance: sequential linear programming on the near-active pairs (sph.maximize), L-BFGS on the smooth maximum of the
cosines at sharpness rising to 1e9 (sph.descend), and basin hopping (sph.improve: shake all points by 1e-4 to 1e-2
of the minimal chord, descend, maximize, keep drops), in passes of a few minutes each, about half an hour per
instance in all. It does not converge: a further minute of descend and maximize still lowers the cosine by up to
about 4e-7 on 24 of the 49. Instances where nothing beat the published code keep it. These codes are the bar the
task scores against. COS2 is the exact signed squared cosine of the binary64 points as numerator/denominator, COORDS
the points as base64 of little-endian binary64 rows. SCiEL run sph1 (Qwen3.8-27B, from these codes) then lowered the
exact cosine of 37 instances, and those codes replace the earlier ones. SCiEL run sphb1 (Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered
39 more, which replace those. SCiEL run sphc1 (Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered 41 more, which replace those."""

